---
title: Fall Fungi (and Friends) at A Fierce Chase
date: 2026-09-15
coverImage: ../../assets/blog/fungi-heading-img.jpg
coverAlt: Turkey tail fungi growing in overlapping fans across a fallen log
draft: true
---

The skis are still in the shed, but the trails are worth walking in September. A
slow lap in the damp after a wet week turns up more than you would expect once
you start looking down.

Turkey tail was everywhere, layered along the fallen logs in overlapping fans.
It is one of the easier ones to recognise and a good place to start if you are
new to looking.

![Bear's head tooth fungus hanging in white cascading spines from a tree branch](../../assets/blog/fungi-bears-head-tooth.jpg)

Bear's head tooth is the showiest thing we found — white cascading spines, hard
to miss once you spot one, and easy to walk straight past if you don't.

![A bolete mushroom with a thick stem growing among moss and forest litter](../../assets/blog/fungi-not-a-porcini.jpg)

This one went in the photo folder as "not a porcini", which is the right
instinct. If you are foraging rather than photographing, identification is on
you — several lookalikes are not worth the risk, and nothing here is advice.

![Orange slime mould fruiting bodies clustered among fallen leaves](../../assets/blog/fungi-slime-mold.jpg)

Slime mould, which is not a fungus at all, and worth the detour to read about.

![Green tree lungwort lichen growing on mossy bark](../../assets/blog/fungi-lichen.jpg)

And lungwort lichen on the bark — a reasonable sign of clean air, which is one
of the quieter arguments for keeping woods like these intact.

The trails are open year-round and the shoulder seasons are the quietest time to
have them to yourself. Watch your footing on the wet leaves.
